Q: How to safely transfer ERC20 ownership to Gnosis Safe without disrupting active validator functions? A: <p>The best way to handle this without downtime is to use a two-step ownership transfer and separate your bridge operational roles from the owner role.If you use a simple single-step transferOwnership, any mistake or typo in the multi-sig address will instantly brick your contract. With a two-step pattern (like OpenZeppelin's Ownable2Step), the current deployer proposes the Gnosis Safe address, but the transfer is only complete when the Gnosis Safe explicitly calls acceptOwnership().Also, keep your validator execution address separate from the contract owner. This way, while the ownership transfer is pending, your validators can keep minting and burning tokens without interruption.Here is a clean way to implement this in Solidity ^0.8.20:</p>
<p>// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT</p>
<p>pragma solidity ^0.8.20;</p>
<p>import &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol&quot;;</p>
<p>import &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable2Step.sol&quot;;</p>
<p>contract WrappedGramBridgeToken is ERC20, Ownable2Step {</p>
<pre><code>address public bridgeExecutionTarget;

event BridgeTargetUpdated(address indexed oldTarget, address indexed newTarget);

constructor(string memory name, string memory symbol) 

    ERC20(name, symbol) 

    Ownable(msg.sender) 

{}

modifier onlyBridge() {

    require(msg.sender == bridgeExecutionTarget, &quot;BridgeToken: Caller is not the authorized bridge&quot;);

    \_;

}

function updateBridgeTarget(address \_newTarget) external onlyOwner {

    require(\_newTarget != address(0), &quot;BridgeToken: New target cannot be zero address&quot;);

    emit BridgeTargetUpdated(bridgeExecutionTarget, \_newTarget);

    bridgeExecutionTarget = \_newTarget;

}

function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyBridge {

    \_mint(to, amount);

}

function burn(address from, uint256 amount) external onlyBridge {

    \_burn(from, amount);

}
</code></pre>
<p>}</p>
<p>Why this works safely:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>No zero-address risk:</strong> <code>Ownable2Step</code> automatically prevents accidental transfers to <code>address(0)</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Zero downtime:</strong> Your validators rely on <code>bridgeExecutionTarget</code> to mint/burn, not the <code>owner</code>. The bridge can keep processing deposits and withdrawals normally while the multi-sig signs the transaction to accept ownership.</p>
</li>
</ul>
